<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="b81b9c74">&lt;p&gt;
    Enablers are planned changes that the project team will institute (build or acquire) that will enable the benefits to
    be driven to realization. Enablers can also be policies, incentives, etc. that are essential to drive the initiative
    into effective operations. Examples are incorporation of a metrics management and reporting tool for creation of
    standardized reports, establish necessary electronic data interchanges, etc.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Dependencies are items outside the scope of the project which could impact the benefits realization schedule. An
    example of a dependency is the project team is dependent on HR creating an employee transition plan.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Benefit triggers are critical flag posts along the benefit horizon, which indicate that the benefits are being realized
    as per the realization plan. Examples of triggers are implementation of a Shared Service Center, automation of an
    activity, etc.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    It is imperative for a well-run benefits realization program to manage these Enablers/Dependencies thoroughly and
    regularly. Monitoring the triggers gives us insights to determine if the benefits will be realized in the time horizon
    or not.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
